    <div class="report-container">
        <header>
            <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</div>
            <div style="margin-top: 15px; opacity: 0.8; font-size: 0.9em;">
                Dataset: 20,058 Games | Source: Lichess.org
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav class="toc">
            <h3>Table of Contents</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><a href="#overview">Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</a></li>
                <li><a href="#color-advantage">White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</a></li>
                <li><a href="#rating-analysis">Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</a></li>
                <li><a href="#opening-strategy">Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</a></li>
                <li><a href="#time-control">Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</a></li>
                <li><a href="#key-findings">Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</a></li>
            </ol>
        </nav>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Dataset Overview -->
        <section id="overview" class="section">
            <h2>1. Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</h2>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card info">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">80.5%</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card success">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Decisive Outcomes</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">95.3%</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card warning">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">4.7%</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Outcome Distribution</h3>
            <p>Of the 20,058 games analyzed, 19,108 (95.3%) resulted in a decisive outcome (white or black victory), while 950 (4.7%) ended in a draw.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="outcomeChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>Victory Status Breakdown</h3>
            <p>Among decisive games, checkmate accounts for <strong>33.1%</strong> of outcomes, making it the second most common decisive end after resignation (58.3%). Time expiry (outoftime) accounts for 8.8% of decisive games.</p>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Victory Status</th>
                        <th>Count</th>
                        <th>Percentage</th>
                        <th>Avg Game Length (Turns)</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Resignation</td>
                        <td>11,147</td>
                        <td>55.6%</td>
                        <td>53.9</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Checkmate</td>
                        <td>6,325</td>
                        <td>31.5%</td>
                        <td>65.4</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Time Expiry</td>
                        <td>1,680</td>
                        <td>8.4%</td>
                        <td>72.7</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Draw</td>
                        <td>906</td>
                        <td>4.5%</td>
                        <td>83.8</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 2: White vs Black -->
        <section id="color-advantage" class="section">
            <h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">The First-Move Advantage, Quantified</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 1.1em;">Across all 19,108 decisive games, <strong>White wins 52.3%</strong> of the time compared to Black's 47.7%. This 4.6 percentage point advantage represents a statistically significant edge that persists across rating levels and time controls, though its magnitude varies considerably.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card success">
                    <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate (Decisive Games)</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">52.3%</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-label">White Wins</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">10,001</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card info">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Black Wins</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">9,107</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card warning">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Advantage Margin</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">+4.6%</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>White's Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>
            <p>Perhaps surprisingly, White's first-move advantage is <em>strongest at higher rating levels</em>. At the 2200+ bracket, White wins 75.9% of decisive games—more than three times the rate at the sub-1200 level (35.7%).</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="ratingBracketChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>White's Advantage by Time Control</h3>
            <p>White maintains a consistent advantage across all major time controls, ranging from 51.1% to 55.3% win rate. Bullet and blitz games (10+0, 5+5) show slightly higher White win rates, possibly due to time pressure effects on Black.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="timeControlChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>White Win Rate by Victory Type</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Victory Status</th>
                        <th>White Win Rate</th>
                        <th>Count</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Checkmate</td>
                        <td>52.9%</td>
                        <td>6,325</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Resignation</td>
                        <td>52.4%</td>
                        <td>11,147</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Time Expiry</td>
                        <td>48.4%</td>
                        <td>1,680</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="finding">
                <div class="finding-title">Key Observation: Time Pressure Favors Black</div>
                <p>When games end by time expiry, White's win rate drops to 48.4%—below 50%. This suggests that under time pressure, Black may have a practical advantage, possibly due to White's obligation to respond to Black's threats immediately after time trouble begins.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Rating Analysis -->
        <section id="rating-analysis" class="section">
            <h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>

            <h3>Rating Distribution</h3>
            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <p><strong>White Player Ratings:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Range: 784 – 2700</li>
                        <li>Mean: 1597</li>
                        <li>Median: 1570</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p><strong>Black Player Ratings:</strong></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Range: 789 – 2723</li>
                        <li>Mean: 1589</li>
                        <li>Median: 1560</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Rating Differential Impact</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 1.1em;">The higher-rated player wins <strong>65.3%</strong> of decisive games overall. This rating advantage effect is substantial and compounds with larger rating gaps—the player with a 500+ rating advantage wins 87.5% of their games.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="ratingDiffChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>Rating Differential Win Rates</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Rating Gap (Absolute)</th>
                        <th>Higher-Rated Player Win Rate</th>
                        <th>Games</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>0-50</td><td>51.6%</td><td>4,992</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>51-100</td><td>58.5%</td><td>3,490</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>101-150</td><td>64.4%</td><td>2,523</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>151-200</td><td>67.1%</td><td>1,832</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>201-300</td><td>75.1%</td><td>2,552</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>301-500</td><td>82.0%</td><td>2,320</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>500+</td><td>87.5%</td><td>1,148</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>Average Game Length by Rating Tier</h3>
            <p>Higher-rated players tend to play longer games, suggesting more positional play and fewer quick blunders. Games at the 2000-2199 level average 67.2 turns, compared to 50.7 turns at the sub-1200 level.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="gameLengthByRatingChart"></canvas>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Opening Analysis -->
        <section id="opening-strategy" class="section">
            <h2>4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>

            <h3>Top 15 Most Played Openings</h3>
            <p>The dataset contains <strong>1,467 distinct ECO codes</strong>, but the top 15 openings account for 14.2% of all games. The most popular opening—Van't Kruijs Opening—appears in only 368 games (1.8% of total), reflecting Lichess's diverse opening repertoire.</p>

